cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Slice packing on a torus pod: what it can still admit, what a chip failure costs, and who is allowed to fix it"
abstract: >-
  Six models for the allocation layer of a torus-connected accelerator pod. A
  topology model in which a slice inherits a closed ring only if it spans that
  dimension completely, so a pod described as a torus hands most jobs a mesh. A
  packing model that replaces the free-chip count with the largest job still
  placeable, and measures how far apart the two drift. A cordon model in which
  the cost of one chip failure depends on where the chip was, ranging from one
  face to half the slice, with a worst case of exactly half that no shape can
  reduce. A re-embedding model that prices shrinking in place, which keeps the
  hardware and breaks the checkpoint, against moving, which keeps the checkpoint
  and costs other tenants. A tenancy model that prices blast-domain isolation
  and identifies a request that isolation cannot satisfy at any price. And a
  reconstitution model that separates what a control plane may do alone from
  what needs a person, and prints what waiting for that person costs.
authors:
  - family-names: Nanyonga
    given-names: Margaret
